  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{x}^{2}-16x+64}{18\\sqrt{x+1}-3x-30}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "If we plug in $x=8$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{x}^{2}-16x+64}{18\\sqrt{x+1}-3x-30} & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}-16x+64]}^{\u2032}}{[18\\sqrt{x+1}-3x-30]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{2x-16}{\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{x+1}}-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{2x-16}{9(x+1)^{-1/2}-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{x}^{2}-16x+64}{18\\sqrt{x+1}-3x-30} & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{2x-16}{9(x+1)^{-1/2}-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{[2x-16]}^{\u2032}}{{[9(x+1)^{-1/2}-3]}^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{2}{-\\frac{9}{2}(x+1)^{-3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}{lim}_{x\\to 8}(x+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}(8+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
